    messages, masked-IP visit statistics, preference cookies), so an author who
    never edits it still has a truthful page rather than boilerplate fiction. %>
<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

<p>This page explains what <%= site_name %> collects when you visit, and what happens to it.</p>

<h2>What's collected</h2>

<p><strong>Your email address, if you give it.</strong> Subscribing to the newsletter records your address and the date you confirmed it. Every subscription is confirmed twice: you ask, we email you a link, and nothing is sent until you click it. Every email carries an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing takes effect immediately.</p>

<p><strong>Anything you write in the contact form.</strong> Messages are stored so they can be read and answered. They aren't added to the newsletter list.</p>

<p><strong>Anonymous visit statistics.</strong> Pages viewed, the site that referred you, and a rough sense of country or region. IP addresses are masked before they're stored and discarded once the location is worked out — the record is never precise enough to identify a household or a person.</p>

<h2>Cookies</h2>

<p>Two kinds, both small: one remembers your reading preferences, such as light or dark mode; the others count visits for the statistics above. None of them follow you to other sites, and there's no advertising network involved.</p>

<h2>What isn't done with it</h2>

<p>Your address is never sold, rented, or traded. It's used to send the newsletter you asked for, and for nothing else.</p>

<h2>Your say</h2>

<p>You can unsubscribe from any email, and you can ask what's held about you or ask for it to be deleted. Write to <%= contact %> and it will be handled.</p>

<h2>Changes</h2>

<p>If this policy changes, the updated version appears on this page.</p>
